f 1033 317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1034 1.4476087343369344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094771 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eeb17fdb-d008-5d2a-befe-3afdce935947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096538 #edb/ref 1069 "e-604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096538 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096538 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({t}^{3}-4t)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [-2,0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{t}^{2}-{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-2\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{8}{3})-2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096561 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096561 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3,\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096561 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{3}(-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{4}{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left( -2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096731 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096758 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096851 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "71e9c379-9817-5da3-85f9-50847626fa5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad65213f-7896-5eb3-8900-a92c0319ea40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097133 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)4t({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097612 #edb/ref 1061 630055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "786032d8-d66a-579e-8336-348ca609cfb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097613 #edb/ref 1061 630060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "296a64b2-a271-5186-bf37-e47d7b56624c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1061 630057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a032caf-6330-5e24-bafb-d45c348ad375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1061 630056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97757635-74ae-5269-ac75-2ea85021adb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad\ny=2t-t^3, \\qquad\nt \\in  (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1,$ as shown. \n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y(t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{3}-3{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1065 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d0c890f-fbe0-5ded-948b-b94d13caac3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! \n\nTo see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0.$ However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0\n$$\n\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1065 7915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89e96b74-e406-52f5-b64e-46267df927e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1067 "A General Formula for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533149 #edb/ref 1080 "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533149 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533149 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533153 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{34}{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533153 #edb/ref 1082 #e