rve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533985 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-1)(3{t}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-7{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{1}-7\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1}+4\\cdot t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{5}-0)-7(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{7}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a886f775367f244f78087935cb717b988d85f1714b2cae7ce3e4ba2525ef178d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533987 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65750eba-d000-52c0-9873-7b35cf42c92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534014 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)=3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15702" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "758e2016-fc3b-5023-8258-63b37b29bb3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534021 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/399578c2a4d43e64be3f811ab0fdf0f5a62456c29d77dc3a822cc0ecea733c77.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2},\\,y=2t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fa9ec9c-84e1-5ae9-97e5-a5fa49b7d98b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534025 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/1628d71e973666b1b79aa5e86a9fa4b7cc8fffec93778bde92db7b3600bd13f5.png)\n\nFind the area enclosed by the curve (shown above) defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,y={t}^{3}-4t,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ef740c3-9ce9-5fcc-84b0-6a5ac9ae7a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534040 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)=2t.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{3}-4t)\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2}-8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{32}{5})-8(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{128}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is $|-\\frac{256}{15}|=\\frac{256}{15}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/0928371c060dc402198b610e7118fdcff68cd0e10c54df43931ff82d2a2697b4.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae8f5be4-7a5b-5146-8905-098ca73c2947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534049 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ and $t\\ge 0.$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi (1)-\\pi (-1))\\,| \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/60f01450ff74239f3f9bedcf9dfc4445f9c557bb52584ee79a0ba61b0d8bb6f7.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3acb4f5-8405-55f7-be88-53d3f71a5599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534062 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)-(t+1)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2)-(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534099 #edb/ref 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{sec}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
